  <p class="article-answer"><strong>The short answer:</strong> Raleigh properties inside city limits that pay stormwater fees may be eligible. As of July 1, 2026, the City says standard approved projects receive 90% cost sharing; qualifying underserved communities, nonprofits and places of worship may receive 100%. Request a consultation and secure approval before construction.</p>

  <h2 id="current">What the current program says</h2>
  <div class="article-table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>City guidance as of August 3, 2026</th></tr></thead><tbody>
    <tr><td>Basic eligibility</td><td>Property is inside Raleigh city limits and pays stormwater fees.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Standard cost share</td><td>The City states that it pays 90% of approved project costs and the resident pays 10%.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Subsidy option</td><td>100% cost share may be available for qualifying underserved communities, property and rental owners, nonprofits and places of worship.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Construction start</td><td>Begin only after the application is approved and the required agreement is complete.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Completion</td><td>The City says applicants have one year to complete an approved project.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ongoing obligation</td><td>A five- or ten-year maintenance agreement and annual maintenance reports are required.</td></tr>
  </tbody></table></div>
  <p>Funding terms and eligibility can change. Confirm them on the City’s application page before relying on this guide. Cost share applies to approved scope and documented costs; it is not an automatic discount on work already completed.</p>

  <h2 id="projects">Projects the program currently lists</h2>
  <ul><li>Rain gardens, with a soil infiltration test before applying.</li><li>Cisterns.</li><li>Downspout disconnection.</li><li>Permeable pavement.</li><li>Impervious-surface removal.</li><li>Green roofs.</li><li>Constructed wetlands.</li></ul>
  <p>The right option depends on the source and volume of runoff, soil, slope, utilities, space and maintenance capacity. A rain garden is not automatically appropriate for a slowly draining low spot, and permeable pavement requires a complete pavement and drainage design.</p>

  <h2 id="process">A homeowner’s application sequence</h2>
  <ol><li><strong>Check the address.</strong> Use the eligibility tool on Raleigh’s Rainwater Rewards page.</li><li><strong>Request a consultation.</strong> City staff discuss the property and potential project, then arrange a site visit.</li><li><strong>Develop the project.</strong> Complete required testing and prepare the design, scope and cost information.</li><li><strong>Submit and wait for approval.</strong> Do not start construction while the application is pending.</li><li><strong>Complete the legal agreement.</strong> The City routes the approved agreement for signatures.</li><li><strong>Build and document.</strong> Keep invoices and construction photographs.</li><li><strong>Request final inspection.</strong> The City confirms the completed work before cost-share payment.</li><li><strong>Maintain and report.</strong> Submit the annual maintenance report for the agreed term.</li></ol>

  <h2 id="timing">How long approval can take</h2>
  <p>The City’s current process table estimates about two weeks for application approval up to $10,000, six weeks from $10,000.01 to $30,000 and ten weeks above $30,000. It separately estimates about four weeks for the legal agreement, one week for final inspection and three weeks for reimbursement. Construction time varies. Treat these as program estimates, not guaranteed dates.</p>
  <aside class="article-note"><strong>Budget for the cash flow</strong><p>The published sequence describes payment after construction, invoices and final inspection. Ask the City and contractor when each payment is due and which costs are reimbursable before signing a contract. The City also notes that recipients may receive a 1099 and are responsible for tax reporting.</p></aside>

  <h2 id="questions">Questions to answer before applying</h2>
  <ol><li>Which roof, driveway or walk is producing the runoff?</li><li>Where does that water go now, and what is downstream?</li><li>What testing and professional design will this project require?</li><li>Which line items are eligible for cost share?</li><li>Who will maintain inlets, plants, sediment, pavement joints or cistern equipment?</li><li>Can the project remain functional for the full agreement term?</li></ol>

